# How the parser pairs a bracket's opener with its closer, by the lexer's own
# stack (design.md §4.17; defect 131's class; split out of `parse/unclosed`
# on 2026-10-01, the recovery batches' C3, when the pairing came to read the
# kinds of the openers it walks past). At the top of `selfhost/` since
# 2026-10-05 (defect 344), out of `parse/`: it reads the tokens, the cursor
# and the lexer's offsets and nothing of the parser, the seam the parser's
# budget lets a move pay along (`tests/harness/suite_layout.hero`, `BUDGETS`).
#
# The lexer keeps every opener on one stack and names the ones it found
# never closed; the parser, reading the same tokens, must pair the same, or
# it would name a closer the lexer already named missing, or read lines the
# lexer closed a bracket before as inside it. So it walks the tokens from an
# opener as the lexer walked them, and where the lexer named what was open,
# at a declaration's line, at a reach's end, at a lost quote's line end or at
# a closer of another kind (`cursor.Cursor.closes`), it stops where the lexer
# did. A leaf: it reads the tokens and nothing that reads the parser.
#
# **Every opener is paired in one walk where the parse's cursor is paired**
# (`pair_all`, defect 344). The parser asks for an opener's closer several
# times, once per element of a list among them, and a walk from the opener
# to its closer, or to where the lexer named it open, cost the square of the
# openers: 2,000 lines of `x = [1, 2` 47.6 billion instructions retired and
# a clean list of 2,000 elements 7.3. One walk keeping the lexer's stack
# pairs them all, each closer popping the last opener, a closer of another
# kind at a named offset popping to its own, a named offset anything else
# emptying the stack, as the walk from one opener does for that opener; the
# test below holds the two to agree on every opener of every text it reads.
#
# | entry | called by |
# |---|---|
# | `paired_closer` | `parse/unclosed.never_closed`, `parse/unclosed.recover_past_pair` |
# | `paired_by_kinds` | `paired_closer`, past a closer of another kind |
# | `close_after` | `parse/unclosed.in_reach`, `parse/brace_habit.closer` |
# | `pair_all` | `parse.parse_source`, `parse.parse_bounded`, `parse/readings.read_apart` |

use token
use cursor
use literals
use lexer
use text_outline

# The index of the closer that closes the opener at token `open`, as the
# lexer's stack pairs them, whatever its kind, or -1 where none does: every
# opener pushed, every closer popping the last one, within the file the
# opener stands in. The author's text ends at `root_end`, where the lexer
# ended its file and named what was left open; and the lexer named every
# opener still open at a declaration opening its line at column 0, at a
# line a reach ends at, and at a line a lost quote ended
# (`cursor.Cursor.closes`), so a closer past one closes nothing before it;
# a closer at such an offset is one of another kind than the innermost,
# which closed the opener of its own kind below and named those above it
# (`closers.hero`, 2026-10-01). A literal that never closed took the closers
# on the rest of its line (`literals.closers_taken`), popped as the lexer
# popped them: an opener one of those closed has no closer the parser can
# take.
function paired_closer(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    if c.tables.pairs.len() == c.tokens.len() && opens(c.tokens[open].kind)
        return c.tables.pairs[open]
    return walked_closer(c, text, open: open)

# The walk from the opener at token `open` alone, `paired_closer`'s answer
# for a cursor `pair_all` did not pair, a view or a test's.
function walked_closer(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    depth: i64 @ 0
    i: i64 @ open
    bounded = c.root_end >= 0 && c.tokens[open].span.start < c.root_end
    closed = close_after(c, at: c.tokens[open].span.start)

    while c.tokens[i].kind != .eof && !(bounded && c.tokens[i].span.start >= c.root_end)
        k = c.tokens[i].kind
        at = c.tokens[i].span.start

        if closed >= 0 && at >= closed
            # A closer of another kind the lexer paired below the innermost
            # takes the walk that knows the kinds; anything else there is
            # where the lexer named every opener open.
            if at == closed && token.is_closer(k)
                return paired_by_kinds(c, text, open: open)
            return -1

        if k == .lparen || k == .lbracket || k == .lbrace
            depth @ depth + 1
        else if token.is_closer(k)
            depth @ depth - 1

            if depth == 0
                return i
        else if k == .error
            depth @ depth - literals.closers_taken(text, c.tokens[i].span)

            if depth <= 0
                return -1
        i @ i + 1

    return -1

# The same walk keeping the kind of every opener open, which the walk by
# depth hands over to at the first closer the lexer paired with an opener
# of its own kind below the innermost: up to there the two pair alike, every
# closer popping the last opener, and from there this one pops to the
# closer's own kind. Kept apart because it builds an array the walk by depth,
# taken for every bracket the parser reads, does not.
function paired_by_kinds(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str, open: i64) -> i64
    kinds: [token.TokenKind] @ [c.tokens[open].kind]
    i: i64 @ open + 1
    bounded = c.root_end >= 0 && c.tokens[open].span.start < c.root_end
    closed: i64 @ close_after(c, at: c.tokens[open].span.start)

    while c.tokens[i].kind != .eof && !(bounded && c.tokens[i].span.start >= c.root_end)
        k = c.tokens[i].kind
        at = c.tokens[i].span.start

        if closed >= 0 && at >= closed
            if at > closed || !token.is_closer(k)
                return -1
            own = innermost(kinds, opener: token.opener_of(k))

            if own < 0
                return -1

            if own == 0
                return i
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: own)
            closed @ close_after(c, at: at)
        else if k == .lparen || k == .lbracket || k == .lbrace
            kinds @ kinds.push(k)
        else if token.is_closer(k)
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: kinds.len() - 1)

            if kinds.len() == 0
                return i
        else if k == .error
            taken = literals.closers_taken(text, c.tokens[i].span)

            if taken >= kinds.len()
                return -1
            kinds @ kinds.slice(from: 0, to: kinds.len() - taken)
        i @ i + 1

    return -1

## Every opener at once

# Pairs every opener of the cursor's stream (`stream_tables.Tables`), so that
# `paired_closer` is a lookup. A cursor this did not pair, a view or a test's,
# holds no table, and each opener is walked from.
function pair_all(@c: cursor.Cursor, text: str)
    c.tables.pairs @ table(c, text)

# The closer of every opener by token index, -1 for one none closes and for
# every token that opens nothing: the lexer's stack kept over the stream once.
# Where the author's text ends at `root_end`, no opener in it is closed past
# it; at an offset where the lexer named the brackets still open, a closer of
# another kind closes the innermost opener of its own kind, those above it
# closed by none, and anything else there closes none of them; a literal that
# never closed pops the openers whose closers it took. Each is the walk from
# one opener (`walked_closer`, `paired_by_kinds`) asked of all at once.
function table(c: cursor.Cursor, text: str) -> [i64]
    pairs: [i64] @ []

    for t in c.tokens
        _ = t
        pairs @ pairs.push(-1)

    stack: [i64] @ []
    next: i64 @ 0
    bounded: bool @ c.root_end >= 0
    i: i64 @ 0

    while c.tokens[i].kind != .eof
        k = c.tokens[i].kind
        at = c.tokens[i].span.start
        taken: bool @ false

        if bounded && at >= c.root_end
            stack @ []
            bounded @ false

        while next < c.closes.len() && c.closes[next] <= at
            if c.closes[next] == at && token.is_closer(k)
                own = innermost_of(c, stack: stack, opener: token.opener_of(k))

                if own >= 0
                    pairs[stack[own]] @ i
                    stack @ stack.slice(from: 0, to: own)
                else
                    stack @ []
                taken @ true
            else
                stack @ []
            next @ next + 1

        if taken
            i @ i + 1
            continue

        if opens(k)
            stack @ stack.push(i)
        else if token.is_closer(k)
            if stack.len() > 0
                pairs[stack[stack.len() - 1]] @ i
                stack @ stack.slice(from: 0, to: stack.len() - 1)
        else if k == .error
            gone = literals.closers_taken(text, c.tokens[i].span)

            if gone >= stack.len()
                stack @ []
            else if gone > 0
                stack @ stack.slice(from: 0, to: stack.len() - gone)
        i @ i + 1

    return pairs

# The position in `stack`, the openers open by token index, of the innermost
# `opener`, or -1.
function innermost_of(c: cursor.Cursor, stack: [i64], opener: token.TokenKind) -> i64
    at: i64 @ stack.len() - 1

    while at >= 0 && c.tokens[stack[at]].kind != opener
        at @ at - 1

    return at

function opens(kind: token.TokenKind) -> bool
    return kind == .lparen || kind == .lbracket || kind == .lbrace

# The index in `kinds`, the openers open in a pairing walk, of the innermost
# `opener`, or -1.
function innermost(kinds: [token.TokenKind], opener: token.TokenKind) -> i64
    at: i64 @ kinds.len() - 1

    while at >= 0 && kinds[at] != opener
        at @ at - 1

    return at

# The first offset past `at` where the lexer named the brackets still open,
# or -1 where it named none there, found by a search: the offsets ascend, each
# named where the lexer stood as it read on (the test below; defect 266).
function close_after(c: cursor.Cursor, at: i64) -> i64
    next = text_outline.last_at_most(c.closes, at: at) + 1

    if next < c.closes.len()
        return c.closes[next]
    return -1

## Tests

# The recovery batches' C3: a closer of another kind than the innermost
# opener closes the one of its own kind below it, the lexer naming the ones
# above never closed at the closer's offset, and the parser pairs the same.
test "a closer of another kind closes the opener of its own kind, and the one above it stays open"
    text = "f(a, [b, c)\nx = [1, 2)\n"
    out = lexer.lex_one(text)
    assert out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert out.diagnostics[0].span.start == 5
    assert out.closes == [10]
    c = cursor.lexed_cursor(tokens: out.tokens, root_end: -1, closes: out.closes, swaps: out.swaps)
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 1) == 8
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 4) < 0

    # `x = [1, 2)`: no `(` is open, so its `)` closes the `[` as before.
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 12) == 16

# Defect 344: the walk from each opener and the one walk over every opener
# agree on every opener of texts that hold each event the walk meets: a
# closer of another kind, one that closes the innermost written in the wrong
# kind, an opener a declaration ended, a literal that never closed and took
# the closers of its line, a body's braces, a hole, a stray closer, and the
# author's text ending inside a bracket.
test "every opener is paired by the one walk as by the walk from it"
    texts = [
        "f(a, [b, c)\nx = [1, 2)\n"
        "f(a, [b, c)\n)\n"
        "function a() {\n    g(1\n}\nfunction b()\n    f(a, [b, c)\n    x = [1, 2\nfunction c()\n    assert report(\") == \"lines: 0\"\n"
        "x = f(\"abc)\nprint(1) )\n"
        "function f()\n    x = [1, 2\n\nfunction main()\n    print(1) )\n"
        "x = [[1, 2\nprint(x)\ny = 3 ))\n"
        "k = f({1: 2\n    x) )\n"
        "y = f\"{(a + [b)}\" ]\n"
        "print(2]\n[0.5, 0.7)\n)\n"
        "m = {1: (2, [3}\n"
        "for (i = 0; i < f(3; i++\n    print(i)\n"
        "function f(g: (function(i64 -> i64)) -> i64\n    return 1\n"
    ]

    for text in texts
        out = lexer.lex_one(text)

        for root in [-1, text.len() / 2]
            c: cursor.Cursor @ cursor.lexed_cursor(
                tokens: out.tokens,
                root_end: root,
                closes: out.closes,
                swaps: out.swaps
            )
            every = table(c, text)

            for at in range(from: 0, to: c.tokens.len())
                if opens(c.tokens[at].kind)
                    assert every[at] == walked_closer(c, text, open: at)
                else
                    assert every[at] == -1

# The premise `close_after`'s search rests on (defect 266): the lexer names
# the brackets still open at offsets that ascend, each where it stands as it
# reads on, at all four of its sites: a body's `}` with a bracket open in it
# (`scan.finish`), a closer of another kind (`closers.close_to_its_kind`), a
# declaration at column 0 (`bracket_reach.ends_at`) and a lost quote's line
# end (`lost_quote`). A site that names an offset behind the lexer fails here,
# and `close_after` with it, which finds the first past an offset by search.
test "the lexer names the brackets still open at offsets that ascend, which close_after searches"
    text = "function a() {\n    g(1\n}\nfunction b()\n    f(a, [b, c)\n    x = [1, 2\nfunction c()\n    assert report(\") == \"lines: 0\"\n"
    out = lexer.lex_one(text)
    assert out.closes.len() == 4

    for at in range(from: 1, to: out.closes.len())
        assert out.closes[at - 1] < out.closes[at]

    c = cursor.lexed_cursor(tokens: out.tokens, root_end: -1, closes: out.closes, swaps: out.swaps)
    assert close_after(c, at: -1) == out.closes[0]
    assert close_after(c, at: out.closes[1]) == out.closes[2]
    assert close_after(c, at: out.closes[3]) == -1

# Defect 300: a function type's `)` left out before its `->` is written
# there by the lexer, zero wide and told once, so the parameters' `(` pairs
# with it and the `)` past the result with the bracket the type stands in.
test "a function type's `)` left out before its `->` pairs its parameters' `(`"
    text = "function f(g: (function(i64 -> i64)) -> i64\n    return 1\n"
    out = lexer.lex_one(text)
    assert out.diagnostics.len() == 1
    assert out.diagnostics[0].code == "expected_function_type_params_close"
    assert out.diagnostics[0].span.start == 28
    c = cursor.lexed_cursor(tokens: out.tokens, root_end: -1, closes: out.closes, swaps: out.swaps)
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 7) == 9
    assert c.tokens[9].span.start == 28 && c.tokens[9].span.end == 28
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 5) == 12
    assert paired_closer(c, text, open: 2) == 13
